IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ap-dates`
IMPORT `ap-duties`

-- Administration and Probate Act 1958 (Vic) -- s 29 (effect of death on causes of action), the s 3 definition of
-- dust-related condition with the First Schedule, s 99AC, and s 30 (the personal representative's notice to a claimant).
--
-- This module holds the law. The questions are asked in ap-seat.l4, which imports it and never restates a rule.
--
-- ANSWERS. A yes/no rule answers JUST TRUE; JUST FALSE where the text's own words say no ("shall not apply", "shall not
-- include", "no proceedings ... may be maintained ... except", a "means" definition, an "if" or "where" that fails); or
-- NOTHING where the text does not answer on the facts given, with a "NOTHING-kind" comment. The Court's judgments ("just and
-- reasonable", "satisfied") are inputs: the answer is conditional on them, never a guess at them.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL VERSION: quotations from a plain-text conversion by Legalese Commons of the Word file of the Administration
-- and Probate Act 1958, Version No. 130 (22 October 2025), not the authorised version. (c) State of Victoria; published with
-- the authority of the Government Printer for the State of Victoria. No part may be reproduced except in accordance with
-- the Copyright Act 1968 (Cth). Quoted under the owner's ruling of 24 October 2026 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Section 29 -- causes of action on a death`

§§ `s 3 and the First Schedule -- dust-related condition`

-- s 3(1): "dust-related condition means -- (a) a disease specified in the First Schedule; or (b) any other pathological
-- condition of the lungs, pleura, peritoneum or sinus that is attributable to dust". The First Schedule's fourteen diseases.
DECLARE `Condition` IS ONE OF
    `Aluminosis`
    `Asbestosis`
    `Asbestos induced carcinoma`
    `Asbestos related pleural diseases`
    `Bagossosis`
    `Berylliosis`
    `Byssinosis`
    `Coal dust pneumoconiosis`
    `Farmers' lung`
    `Hard metal pneumoconiosis`
    `Mesothelioma`
    `Silicosis`
    `Silico-tuberculosis`
    `Talcosis`
    `another pathological condition of the lungs, pleura, peritoneum or sinus`
    `a condition of another kind`

-- A "means" definition: where neither paragraph holds, the answer is no.
GIVEN `the condition` IS A `Condition`
      `the condition is attributable to dust` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 3 -- a dust-related condition` `the condition` `the condition is attributable to dust` IF
    CONSIDER `the condition`
    WHEN `another pathological condition of the lungs, pleura, peritoneum or sinus` THEN `the condition is attributable to dust`
    WHEN `a condition of another kind` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `s 29(1) -- which causes of action survive`

-- s 29(1): "on the death of any person, all causes of action subsisting against or vested in him shall survive against or
-- (as the case may be) for the benefit of his estate: Provided that this subsection shall not apply to causes of action for
-- defamation or seduction or for inducing one spouse to leave or remain apart from the other." s 29(5): the section applies
-- to causes of action under Part III of the Wrongs Act 1958 (the dependants' claim) as to others.
DECLARE `Kind of cause of action` IS ONE OF
    `a claim for injury, loss or damage of any other kind`
    `a dependants' claim under Part III of the Wrongs Act 1958`
    `defamation`
    `seduction`
    `inducing one spouse to leave or remain apart from the other`

GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of cause of action`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29(1) -- the cause of action survives the death` `the kind` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the kind`
    WHEN `defamation` THEN JUST FALSE
    WHEN `seduction` THEN JUST FALSE
    WHEN `inducing one spouse to leave or remain apart from the other` THEN JUST FALSE
    OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§§ `s 29(2), (2A) and s 99AC -- what the estate may recover`

DECLARE `Head of damages` IS ONE OF
    `exemplary damages`
    `funeral expenses`
    `a loss or gain to the estate consequent on the death`
    `pain or suffering`
    `bodily or mental harm`
    `the curtailment of expectation of life`
    `future probable earnings had the person survived`
    `loss of earning capacity for a period after the death`
    `any other loss suffered before the death`

DECLARE `What s 29 says of a head of damages` IS ONE OF
    `the cause of action did not survive, so nothing is recoverable for the estate`
    `included`
    `may be included`
    `not included`
    `s 29 does not limit it: the general law of damages decides`

-- s 29(2A) as limited by s 99AC: "Section 29(2A) does not apply to a cause of action, proceedings in respect of which
-- commenced before the commencement of section 4 of the Administration and Probate (Dust Diseases) Act 2000, if the plaintiff
-- has died before the commencement of section 4 of that Act." The endnotes give that Act's commencement as 10 May 2000
-- (No. 15/2000, "Commencement Date: 10.5.00").
`s 4 of the Administration and Probate (Dust Diseases) Act 2000 commenced on` MEANS `the calendar date` 10 5 2000

GIVEN `the death was caused by the act or omission` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the condition the person died from` IS A `Condition`
      `the condition is attributable to dust` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the condition was caused by the act or omission` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person had commenced proceedings that were pending at death` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day proceedings were commenced` IS A DATE
      `the day of death` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 29(2A) -- the dust disease exception applies` `the death was caused by the act or omission` `the condition the person died from` `the condition is attributable to dust` `the condition was caused by the act or omission` `the person had commenced proceedings that were pending at death` `the day proceedings were commenced` `the day of death` IF
        `the death was caused by the act or omission`
    AND `s 3 -- a dust-related condition` `the condition the person died from` `the condition is attributable to dust`
    AND `the condition was caused by the act or omission`
    AND `the person had commenced proceedings that were pending at death`
    AND (NOT (    (`the day proceedings were commenced` `is before` `s 4 of the Administration and Probate (Dust Diseases) Act 2000 commenced on`)
              AND (`the day of death` `is before` `s 4 of the Administration and Probate (Dust Diseases) Act 2000 commenced on`)))

-- s 29(2): "the damages recoverable for the benefit of the estate of that person -- (a) shall not include any exemplary
-- damages; ... (c) where the death of that person has been caused by the act or omission which gives rise to the cause of
-- action -- (i) shall be calculated without reference to any loss or gain to his estate consequent on his death, except that
-- a sum in respect of funeral expenses may be included; (ii) shall not, except as provided in subsection (2A), include any
-- damages for his pain or suffering or for any bodily or mental harm suffered by him or for the curtailment of his
-- expectation of life; (iii) shall be calculated without reference to the future probable earnings of the deceased had he
-- survived and without any allowance for the loss of his earning capacity that relates to any period after his death."
-- (2A): where the death is from a dust-related condition caused by the act or omission and proceedings commenced by the
-- person were pending at death, the damages "shall include" (d) pain or suffering, (e) bodily or mental harm, (f) the
-- curtailment of expectation of life. s 29(2)(b) (breach of promise of marriage) is out (NOTES.md).
GIVEN `the kind` IS A `Kind of cause of action`
      `the head` IS A `Head of damages`
      `the death was caused by the act or omission` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the dust disease exception applies` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `What s 29 says of a head of damages`
`s 29(2) -- what the estate may recover for` `the kind` `the head` `the death was caused by the act or omission` `the dust disease exception applies` MEANS
    IF NOT (`s 29(1) -- the cause of action survives the death` `the kind` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
    THEN `the cause of action did not survive, so nothing is recoverable for the estate`
    ELSE CONSIDER `the head`
         WHEN `exemplary damages` THEN `not included`
         WHEN `any other loss suffered before the death` THEN `s 29 does not limit it: the general law of damages decides`
         OTHERWISE
            IF NOT `the death was caused by the act or omission`
            THEN `s 29 does not limit it: the general law of damages decides`
            ELSE CONSIDER `the head`
                 WHEN `funeral expenses` THEN `may be included`
                 WHEN `pain or suffering` THEN `the non-economic heads`
                 WHEN `bodily or mental harm` THEN `the non-economic heads`
                 WHEN `the curtailment of expectation of life` THEN `the non-economic heads`
                 OTHERWISE `not included`
    WHERE
        `the non-economic heads` MEANS
            IF `the dust disease exception applies` THEN `included` ELSE `not included`

§§ `s 29(3), (4) -- suing the estate of a person who has died`

-- s 29(3): "Where a cause of action in tort survives against the estate of a deceased person ... no proceedings in respect of
-- the cause of action may be maintained against the estate of the deceased person except proceedings -- (a) that were
-- commenced against the deceased before his death and were pending and not barred at the date of his death; (b) that are
-- commenced against his personal representative after his death -- (i) within the period within which those proceedings
-- might have been commenced against him had he lived; or (ii) where the proceedings are proceedings for negligence nuisance
-- or breach of duty ... and the damages claimed ... include damages in respect of personal injuries to any person, within
-- such longer period as a court, on application made to it by the claimant ... decides is just and reasonable; (c) that were
-- not barred at the date of his death and are commenced against his personal representative within six months after his
-- personal representative takes out representation."
-- s 29(4) (damage suffered at or after the death is treated as if the cause of action subsisted before it) is why the facts
-- below do not ask when the damage was suffered.
GIVEN `the cause of action is in tort` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I commenced proceedings before the death, pending and not barred at the death` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my last day had the person lived` IS A DATE
      `my claim was barred at the death` IS A BOOLEAN
      `representation has been taken out` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day representation was taken out` IS A DATE
      `the claim is for personal injuries in negligence, nuisance or breach of duty` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a court has allowed a longer period` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the last day of the longer period` IS A DATE
      `the day I commence` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 29(3) -- I may maintain proceedings against the estate` `the cause of action is in tort` `I commenced proceedings before the death, pending and not barred at the death` `my last day had the person lived` `my claim was barred at the death` `representation has been taken out` `the day representation was taken out` `the claim is for personal injuries in negligence, nuisance or breach of duty` `a court has allowed a longer period` `the last day of the longer period` `the day I commence` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT `the cause of action is in tort` THEN NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording: s 29(3) speaks only of a cause of action in tort)
           IF `I commenced proceedings before the death, pending and not barred at the death` THEN JUST TRUE
           IF `the day I commence` `is on or before` `my last day had the person lived` THEN JUST TRUE
           IF     `the claim is for personal injuries in negligence, nuisance or breach of duty`
              AND `a court has allowed a longer period`
              AND (`the day I commence` `is on or before` `the last day of the longer period`)
           THEN JUST TRUE
           IF     (NOT `my claim was barred at the death`)
              AND `representation has been taken out`
              AND (`the day I commence` `is on or before` (`the date` 6 `months after` `the day representation was taken out`))
           THEN JUST TRUE
           OTHERWISE JUST FALSE

DECLARE `Last day to sue the estate` IS ONE OF
    `the last day is` HAS `the day` IS A DATE
    `six months after representation is taken out, and at least until` HAS `the earliest last day` IS A DATE

-- The last day under s 29(3)(b)(i) and (c), without a court's longer period under (b)(ii) (fork F2).
GIVEN `my last day had the person lived` IS A DATE
      `my claim was barred at the death` IS A BOOLEAN
      `representation has been taken out` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day representation was taken out` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `Last day to sue the estate`
`s 29(3) -- my last day to sue the estate` `my last day had the person lived` `my claim was barred at the death` `representation has been taken out` `the day representation was taken out` MEANS
    IF `my claim was barred at the death`
    THEN `the last day is` `my last day had the person lived`
    ELSE IF `representation has been taken out`
         THEN `the last day is` (`the later of` `my last day had the person lived` `and` (`the date` 6 `months after` `the day representation was taken out`))
         ELSE `six months after representation is taken out, and at least until` `my last day had the person lived`

§ `Section 30 -- the personal representative's notice to a claimant`

-- s 30(1): three months "from the date of receiving such notice" (fork F1 for the month count).
GIVEN `the day I received the notice` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 30 -- the last day to take proceedings after the notice` `the day I received the notice` MEANS
    `the date` 3 `months after` `the day I received the notice`

GIVEN `the day I received the notice` IS A DATE
      `I have taken proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day I took proceedings` IS A DATE
      `today` IS A DATE
GIVETH A `What became of the step`
`s 30 -- what became of my proceedings after the notice` `the day I received the notice` `I have taken proceedings` `the day I took proceedings` `today` MEANS
    `the outcome of` (`s 30 -- the claimant must take proceedings within three months of the notice` (`the days from` `the day I received the notice` `to` (`s 30 -- the last day to take proceedings after the notice` `the day I received the notice`))) `by` `the claimant` `with` `take all proceedings proper to enforce or establish the claim` `counted from` `the day I received the notice` `done` `I have taken proceedings` `on` `the day I took proceedings` `today`
